Glide64mk2: WaitForGPU, whether the GPU is waited for at each frame.

grBufferSwap starts with a glFinish: the CPU waits for the GPU to be done with
the frame before it swaps, and cannot start the next one meanwhile, so the two
take turns instead of working at the same time. On a SoC where both are close
to their limit (Tegra X1, Nintendo Switch) the sum of their times can go over
the frame time and make the game run in halves of the speed. With WaitForGPU 0
(Video-Glide64mk2) the glFinish is not done and the driver queues the frames
(the picture is the same, as OpenGL runs its commands in order; the latency may
grow by a frame). The default, 1, is what the plugin always did. OpenGL ES builds
ignore it.

diff --git a/src/Glide64/Main.cpp b/src/Glide64/Main.cpp
index a0c13e9..25a29b3 100644
--- a/src/Glide64/Main.cpp
+++ b/src/Glide64/Main.cpp
@@ -400,6 +400,7 @@ void ReadSettings ()
   Config_ReadInt("RenderHeight", "Height the game is drawn at, scaled to the window (0: the window's)", 0, TRUE, FALSE);
   Config_ReadInt("OverscanHorizontal", "Pixels of the picture cropped on the left and on the right, 0 to 20", 0, TRUE, FALSE);
   Config_ReadInt("OverscanVertical", "Pixels of the picture cropped on the top and on the bottom, 0 to 20", 0, TRUE, FALSE);
+  Config_ReadInt("WaitForGPU", "Wait for the GPU to finish each frame before showing it (it may cost speed in slow GPUs, and lowers the latency)", 1, TRUE, TRUE);
   settings.scr_res_x = settings.res_x = Config_ReadScreenInt("ScreenWidth");
   settings.scr_res_y = settings.res_y = Config_ReadScreenInt("ScreenHeight");
 
